docs:
Installed `UI Sounds`. The React API is in `@/components/ui/ui-sounds` (`SoundProvider`, `useSound`, `usePatch`, `useSequence`, `useSoundControls`) and a ready-made mute switch and volume slider is `SoundControls` in `@/components/ui/ui-sounds-controls`. The engine, effects, patch loader, sequencer, analyser and offline renderer are in `@/lib/ui-sounds-*` (`@/lib/ui-sounds-api` is the vanilla entry), and the 62-sound Core pack is data in `@/lib/ui-sounds-core-patch`. No audio files, no network.

```tsx
import { SoundProvider, usePatch } from "@/components/ui/ui-sounds";
import { SoundControls } from "@/components/ui/ui-sounds-controls";
import { corePatch } from "@/lib/ui-sounds-core-patch";

function SaveButton() {
  const patch = usePatch(corePatch);
  return (
    <button type="button" onClick={() => patch.play("success")}>
      Save
    </button>
  );
}

<SoundProvider enabled={on} volume={volume} onEnabledChange={setOn} onVolumeChange={setVolume}>
  <SoundControls />
  <SaveButton />
</SoundProvider>
```

Sounds start only from a handler you call, so the browser's autoplay rules are met. Keep `enabled` and `volume` in your own state (the provider is controlled) and offer the switch to your users. By default the hooks stay silent while the user prefers reduced motion, as upstream does; `SoundControls` says so and `respectReducedMotion={false}` lets your own control decide. `useAnalyser` creates the audio context while rendering, so use it only in a client-only subtree.

Ported from the declarative audio library by Raphael Salaja (MIT); the license notice is at the end of every file.

